+ * Many applets parse only short options, so a GNU-style command line such as
+ * "du --max-depth=1" or "grep --line-number" is rejected even when the applet
+ * has exactly the right short option.  Rather than adding a getopt32long()
+ * table to every applet (and depending on every applet's optstring layout),
+ * this table maps a long name to the short letter it stands for.  Whether the
+ * letter takes an argument is NOT recorded here: it is looked up in the
+ * applet's own optstring at run time, so a row can never be out of sync with
+ * the applet.
+ *
+ * Rules for adding a row:
+ *  - only where the letter means what the GNU long name means.  "ls -C"
+ *    colorizes here but selects columns in GNU, so --format/--color are not
+ *    rows.  A row that means something slightly different is worse than no row.
+ *  - the long name must be the one GNU documents; agents type GNU names.
+ */
